  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday night through Wednesday)\nIssued at 150 AM MDT Thu Aug 27 2026\n\nStorms will taper off Friday night. On Saturday, the upper high\nshifts over east central NM, but also elongates eastward across \nmuch of OK. This shift combined with a trough over the CA coast\nshould allow a better moisture plume to stand up over AZ and\nwestern NM. Thus, storms on Saturday afternoon should favor\nwestern NM, and with PWATs approaching one inch, will be capable\nof heavy rainfall and localized nuisance or flash flooding. Storms\nwill be moving toward the northeast around 10 to 15 mph. Isolated\nto scattered storms will also develop along the Central Mountain \nChain but the focus will really be across western NM. Instead,\ntemperatures will climb across eastern NM with the upper high\noverhead. High temperatures will near records as they soar up to\n12 degrees above normal. \n\nThe upper high will finally shift east of NM Saturday night into\nSunday. Models are hinting at a vort max, presumably from \nconvection over MX the previous evening/night, will cross \nnorthwest NM Saturday night keeping showers and thunderstorms \ngoing through the overnight period. Timing will be everything with\nthat vort max. If it does cross overnight and keep precipitation\ngoing, debris clouds may struggle to clear and the atmosphere\ncould stay rather stable Sunday across the northwest. If the vort\nmax crosses earlier or later, there will be better chances for\nstorms Sunday afternoon across western NM. The upper high will\nremain east of NM, but it doesn't want to loosen its grip\nentirely. A ridge axis will be elongated back into east \ncentral/southeast NM which will limit storm potential. Thus, \npresuming there is enough instability, the favored area for storms\nand heavy rainfall will be across the west Sunday. Another vort \nmax and/or energy from the original CA coast trough will cross NW \nNM Sunday night into Monday. The elongation of the ridge weakens \naround 1-2 dam at H5 across eastern NM Monday afternoon. With that\nin mind, storms should reach a little farther east than on \nSunday, favoring areas along and west of the Central Mtn Chain.\n\nModels are struggling with the position of the upper high Tuesday\nand Wednesday, though there is some hint that it will shift back\nover NM or perhaps a secondary high will redevelop over NM. Should\nthat occur, it will likely disrupt the moisture plume and storm\ncoverage will scale back from Sun/Mon.",
